< Fix the lifts as in the proof of Theorem 2.3, so that the exponents $k_i$ are no longer normalised separately for each braid, and say that $\Phi$ has depth $\ge d$ if $\rho_4(\Phi)\equiv I\pmod{(t+1)^d}$. The argument of Lemma~\ref{lem:tminus1}, together with the fact that $t+1$ is a prime element of $\mathbb{Z}[t^{\pm1}]$ that does not divide $1-t^{\pm1}$, shows that $\M_{f(\Phi)\cdot\Gamma}\equiv\M_\Gamma\pmod{(t+1)^d}$ when $\Phi$ has depth $\ge d$. In particular, if $\M_{f(\Phi)\cdot\Gamma}\neq\M_\Gamma$, the exponents of $\M_{f(\Phi)\cdot\Gamma}-\M_\Gamma$ span at least $d$. If both braids satisfy the parity condition, Lemma~\ref{lem:sign} gives $\M(t)=\pm p(-t)$ with $p(s)=\sum_i s^{k_i}$, so the exponent multisets $\{k_i\}$ of $f(\Phi)\cdot\Gamma$ and $\Gamma$ have the same factorial moments $p^{(j)}(1)$ for $j<d$ (the case $j=0$ also forces the two signs to agree); the comparison in Corollary 6.8 is the case $j=0$. Nontrivial Brunnian braids of arbitrarily large depth exist: $\Phi_0$ and $\sigma_3\Phi_0\sigma_3^{-1}$ lie in the free group $\Brun_4$ and do not commute, so the iterated commutators $[\Phi_0,[\Phi_0,\dots,[\Phi_0,\sigma_3\Phi_0\sigma_3^{-1}]\dots]]$ are nontrivial, and each commutator raises the depth by at least one. So a version of Proposition 6.7 that keeps the parity condition for both braids and covers every nontrivial Brunnian braid would have to compare $f(\Phi)\cdot\Gamma$ with $\Gamma$ through a statistic of the exponents that is not determined by their moments up to any order fixed in advance. The span $M_+-M_-$ of the exponents, which the proof of Corollary 6.8 already introduces, is one such statistic.
